/* data/learn-c/chem-12-11.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-12-11.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-11"] = {"11.1":{"s":"Alcohols have an −OH group on a saturated (sp³) carbon. Phenols have an −OH group directly on a benzene ring. Ethers have an oxygen atom joining two carbon groups (R–O–R′). This chapter covers how to name, make and use all three, and how they react.","w":["In a phenol the OH is on a benzene ring. In an alcohol it is on a saturated carbon. Benzyl alcohol is an alcohol, not a phenol.","An ether has an oxygen between two carbon groups; it has no OH."],"x":["Alcohols, phenols and ethers are compounds of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, in which oxygen is bonded by single bonds. They are all derived from water, H–O–H, by replacing hydrogen atoms with carbon groups.","An alcohol has the –OH group on a saturated (sp³) carbon, as in CH₃CH₂OH. A phenol has the –OH group directly on a benzene ring, as in C₆H₅OH. An ether has an oxygen atom between two carbon groups, R–O–R′, as in CH₃OCH₃. An alcohol is made by replacing one H of water, and an ether by replacing both.","This chapter shows how to classify and name these compounds, how they are made, how their structure sets their physical properties (hydrogen bonding), and how they react. A common mistake is to call a compound with –OH on the side chain of a benzene ring a phenol. Benzyl alcohol, C₆H₅CH₂OH, is an alcohol, because the –OH is on a saturated carbon."],"e":"Classify: CH₃CH₂OH is an alcohol. C₆H₅OH is a phenol. C₂H₅OC₂H₅ is an ether. C₆H₅CH₂OH is an alcohol (the –OH is on the CH₂ carbon, not on the ring)."},"11.2":{"s":"By number of −OH groups: monohydric, dihydric (glycol) and trihydric (glycerol). Monohydric alcohols are primary, secondary or tertiary, depending on how many carbons are attached to the carbon carrying the −OH. Ethers are simple (both groups the same) or mixed (different groups).","w":["Primary, secondary or tertiary describes the carbon that carries the −OH, not the number of carbons in the chain."],"x":["By the number of –OH groups, alcohols are monohydric (one –OH, like ethanol), dihydric (two –OH groups, like ethylene glycol, a diol) and trihydric (three –OH groups, like glycerol, a triol). Phenols are classified the same way: phenol itself is monohydric, and catechol and resorcinol are dihydric.","Monohydric alcohols are primary (1°), secondary (2°) or tertiary (3°). The class depends on the carbon that carries the –OH group: a primary alcohol has it on a carbon bonded to one other carbon (or none), a secondary alcohol on a carbon bonded to two other carbons, and a tertiary alcohol on a carbon bonded to three other carbons. Examples: ethanol is 1°, propan-2-ol is 2°, and 2-methylpropan-2-ol is 3°.","Ethers are simple (symmetrical) if both groups are the same, as in diethyl ether, and mixed (unsymmetrical) if the groups are different, as in ethyl methyl ether. The class of an alcohol decides how it reacts with oxidising agents and with HX, so it is important in problems.","Common mistake: using the length of the chain to name the class. The class depends only on the carbon that holds the –OH."],"e":"Class of the alcohols with the formula C₄H₉OH: butan-1-ol is 1°, butan-2-ol is 2°, 2-methylpropan-1-ol is 1°, and 2-methylpropan-2-ol is 3°."},"11.3":{"s":"Common names add 'alcohol' after the alkyl group (methyl alcohol). IUPAC names replace the final -e of the alkane with -ol and number the chain to give the −OH carbon the lowest number (propan-2-ol). Phenol keeps its name, with ring positions numbered. Ethers are named as alkoxyalkanes (methoxyethane).","w":["In IUPAC names the −OH group gets the lowest possible number, even ahead of a double bond."],"x":["Common names. For an alcohol, write the alkyl group and then the word alcohol: methyl alcohol, ethyl alcohol, isopropyl alcohol. For an ether, write the two alkyl groups in alphabetical order and add ether: ethyl methyl ether. Phenol and its derivatives keep traditional names such as cresol, catechol and resorcinol.","IUPAC names of alcohols. Pick the longest chain that carries the –OH group, replace the final -e of the alkane with -ol, and number the chain so that the –OH gets the lowest possible number. The number is written before -ol: propan-2-ol. A dihydric alcohol ends in -diol, as in ethane-1,2-diol. The –OH takes the lowest number even before a double bond.","Phenols are named as derivatives of phenol, with the ring carbon holding the –OH as number 1: 2-methylphenol, 4-nitrophenol. Ortho, meta and para may also be used for positions 2, 3 and 4.","Ethers are named as alkoxyalkanes. The smaller group, with the oxygen, is the alkoxy part, and the longer chain is the parent: CH₃OCH₂CH₃ is methoxyethane, and C₂H₅OC₂H₅ is ethoxyethane. Common mistake: counting the carbon of the alkoxy group in the main chain."],"e":"Name CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃. The chain has four carbons, and the –OH is on carbon 2 when counted from the left end. So the name is butan-2-ol, and the alcohol is secondary."},"11.4":{"s":"Alcohols are made by hydrolysis of alkyl halides, hydration of alkenes, reducing aldehydes and ketones, and Grignard reactions. Phenol is made from chlorobenzene, cumene or diazonium salts. Hydrogen bonding gives them high boiling points; the lower alcohols dissolve in water, but solubility falls as the carbon chain grows. Phenol is more acidic than alcohols because its phenoxide ion is stabilised by resonance. Reactions include esterification, oxidation, dehydration and ring substitution in phenol.","w":["Reactivity of alcohols towards HX is 3° > 2° > 1°.","Phenol is acidic enough to react with NaOH, but not with sodium bicarbonate."],"x":["Making alcohols. (1) Hydrolysis of an alkyl halide with aqueous KOH. (2) Hydration of an alkene with water and an acid catalyst, which follows Markovnikov's rule. (3) Reduction of aldehydes (to primary alcohols) and ketones (to secondary alcohols) with NaBH₄, LiAlH₄ or H₂ over nickel. (4) Grignard reagents: methanal gives a primary alcohol, other aldehydes give secondary alcohols, and ketones give tertiary alcohols. Ethanol is made industrially by the fermentation of sugars.","Making phenol. From chlorobenzene by heating with NaOH at high temperature and pressure, then acidifying. From the cumene process, in which cumene is oxidised by air and the product is split by acid into phenol and acetone. From a diazonium salt by warming with water.","Physical properties. The –OH group forms hydrogen bonds, so alcohols and phenols boil much higher than hydrocarbons or ethers of similar mass. Lower alcohols dissolve in water, and solubility falls as the carbon chain grows.","Reactions of the –OH group. Alcohols react with sodium to give hydrogen (the order of acidity is 1° more than 2° more than 3°), and with acids or acid chlorides to give esters. With HX the order of reactivity is 3° more than 2° more than 1°, which is used in the Lucas test. Heating with concentrated H₂SO₄ gives an alkene at about 443 K and an ether at about 413 K. Oxidation: a primary alcohol gives an aldehyde and then an acid, a secondary alcohol gives a ketone, and a tertiary alcohol resists oxidation.","Phenol. It is more acidic than alcohols because the phenoxide ion is stabilised by resonance. It reacts with NaOH but not with NaHCO₃. The ring is activated for substitution at the ortho and para positions: bromine water gives a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol, and concentrated nitric acid gives picric acid. Phenol gives a violet colour with neutral FeCl₃."],"e":"Which alcohol gives turbidity at once with the Lucas reagent? The tertiary one, 2-methylpropan-2-ol. A secondary alcohol takes about five minutes, and a primary alcohol shows no change at room temperature."},"11.5":{"s":"Ethers are made by dehydrating alcohols with concentrated sulfuric acid at a controlled temperature. Another way is the Williamson synthesis: an alkoxide reacts with an alkyl halide. They have low boiling points because they cannot hydrogen-bond with each other. They are fairly unreactive, but hot HI splits them into an alcohol and an alkyl iodide.","w":["Williamson synthesis works best with a primary alkyl halide; a tertiary halide gives an alkene instead of an ether.","Heating ethanol with concentrated H₂SO₄ gives ether at the lower temperature and ethene at the higher one."],"x":["Ethers have the oxygen bonded to two carbon groups. The C–O–C angle is about 110°, so the molecule is bent and slightly polar. Ethers cannot form hydrogen bonds with each other, so their boiling points are low, close to those of alkanes of similar mass. They can accept hydrogen bonds from water, so the lower ethers are slightly soluble in it.","Making ethers. (1) Dehydration of alcohols: ethanol heated with concentrated H₂SO₄ at about 413 K gives diethyl ether, and at about 443 K gives ethene. (2) Williamson synthesis: an alkoxide ion reacts with an alkyl halide, R–ONa + R′–X gives R–O–R′ + NaX. This is the way to make mixed ethers.","In the Williamson synthesis use a primary alkyl halide. A secondary or tertiary halide gives an alkene by elimination instead of the ether. For an ether like tert-butyl methyl ether, use sodium tert-butoxide only if the halide is methyl, and not the other way round.","Reactions. Ethers are fairly unreactive. The C–O bond is split by hot concentrated HI (or HBr): R–O–R′ + HI gives an alcohol and an alkyl iodide, and excess HI changes the alcohol to the iodide. Ethers also form explosive peroxides on long contact with air, so they are stored carefully."],"e":"Make ethoxypropane. Use sodium ethoxide with 1-bromopropane: C₂H₅ONa + CH₃CH₂CH₂Br gives C₂H₅OCH₂CH₂CH₃ + NaBr. The halide is primary, so substitution wins over elimination."},"11.6":{"s":"Uses. Methanol is a solvent for paints and varnishes. Ethanol is a solvent and an antifreeze, and is used in medicines and as a fuel. Phenol is used to make plastics (Bakelite), drugs and antiseptics. Diethyl ether is a solvent and was once used as an anaesthetic.","w":["Methanol is poisonous, even in small amounts. Denatured ethanol is made undrinkable on purpose.","Phenol is a raw material for Bakelite, drugs and antiseptics."],"x":["Methanol is used as a solvent for paints and varnishes, and to make formaldehyde and other chemicals. It is poisonous, and can cause blindness if drunk. Ethanol is used as a solvent, as an ingredient of medicines, perfumes and cough syrups, as an antiseptic and as a fuel mixed with petrol. Denatured alcohol has been made unfit for drinking by adding poisonous substances.","Glycerol is used in cosmetics, in medicines and to make explosives such as nitroglycerine. Ethylene glycol is used as an antifreeze in car radiators and to make polyester fibres.","Phenol is used to make plastics such as Bakelite, dyes, drugs (aspirin is made from salicylic acid, which comes from phenol) and antiseptics. Dilute solutions of phenol and its derivatives are used as disinfectants. Picric acid is used in dyes and explosives.","Ethers. Diethyl ether is a good solvent for fats and oils, and was used as an anaesthetic. Its vapour is very flammable. Dimethyl ether is used as a fuel and a propellant."],"e":"Why is ethylene glycol added to car radiator water in cold countries? It dissolves in water and lowers the freezing point of the mixture, so the radiator water does not freeze."}};
